      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "# The breaker experiment \u2014 \u00a7XIII\n\n**A-B-B-A at 1,600 rps offered, 3-minute arms, Lambda reserved concurrency 900.**\nShaded regions mark the arms: **red = control (`/db`, unguarded)**, **green = treatment (`/db-breaker`, resilience4j circuit breaker)**, **blue = recovery gates**, which probed at 400 rps and refused to start the next arm below 98% served.\n\n**\u00a7IX predicted** a deliberate shedder would raise goodput *and* cut cost at identical offered load past the knee.\n**Cost: confirmed** \u2014 GB-s per successful request 2.593 \u2192 1.092, replicate ranges do not overlap.\n**Goodput: not established** \u2014 1.26\u00d7 mean, but ranges overlap (B2 355.0/s is worse than A2 411.1/s). A single A1-vs-B1 pair showed 1.90\u00d7 and would have supported a claim the full design does not.\n\n**Panels, in reading order:**\n1. *Billed occupancy* \u2014 the cost mechanism. A Lambda blocked in borrow-wait is billed for the waiting.\n2. *Who sheds* \u2014 the unpredicted result. Breaker arms were invoked **3.6\u00d7 more often** while costing 44% less.\n3. *Aurora saturation* \u2014 the database was hammered in **both** arms. The breaker is not protecting the database; it moves the shed decision from platform to application.\n4. *Pool queueing* \u2014 where pressure actually appears: borrow latency, not connection count (214 \u2192 232 across reservations).\n5. *Admission limit* \u2014 concurrency pinned at 900, which is what makes the arms comparable.\n\n**Reading the gaps.** Lines break where no data exists. Lambda publishes metrics only while invoked, so the recovery gates are genuinely empty, not zero and not a decline.\n\n\u26a0 **The billed-duration distribution panel is absent by necessity.** It read from Logs Insights over `/aws/lambda/lra-database-resilience`, and that log group was deleted with the stack at teardown. Its percentiles (control p50 199 ms / p95 13,529 ms vs breaker p50 23 ms / p95 397 ms) survive only in the write-up and in `loadtest/results/`. CloudWatch *metrics* persist ~15 months and are unaffected \u2014 every panel here resolves against deleted resources."
      }
    },
